cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "CladisticsPro: cladística morfológica en el navegador"
abstract: >-
  CladisticsPro (characters, homology and trees) is a self-contained web application for
  cladistic analysis of morphological characters, from the character matrix to the diagnosed
  cladogram, entirely in the browser, with no installation and no server-side computation.
  It edits the matrix with a statement for each character and a name for each state, with
  polymorphisms, missing data, inapplicable characters and their dependencies, additive
  characters, step matrices, continuous characters, and a picture for each state and each
  taxon; it reads and writes NEXUS, xread, CSV and TSV and checks the matrix before the
  analysis. It finds the most parsimonious trees by exact branch and bound or by Wagner
  addition with TBR, the parsimony ratchet, sectorial search, tree drifting and tree fusing,
  with group constraints and in parallel Web Workers, treating inapplicable data with the
  algorithm of Brazeau, Guillerme and Smith (2019). It weights characters by implied weights,
  extended implied weights and successive weighting, with the sensitivity of the result to the
  concavity constant. It draws every change on its branch (unique or homoplastic) under
  ACCTRAN, DELTRAN or unambiguous optimization, paints the tree by a character and lets the
  cladogram be edited with the mouse while its length is recomputed; it diagnoses each
  character (steps, CI, RI, RC) and each clade by its synapomorphies; it measures support by
  absolute and relative Bremer support, bootstrap, jackknife, symmetric resampling and GC; it
  summarizes the shortest trees by strict, majority-rule, extended majority-rule, semistrict
  and Adams consensus, with reduced consensus and wildcard taxa; and it tests hypotheses of
  monophyly or whole trees by their cost in steps with the Templeton, winning-sites and
  Kishino–Hasegawa tests. It ends in a publication cladogram with the pictures of the taxa,
  every figure in SVG, trees in NEXUS, xread and Newick, a methods section written in Spanish
  or English from what was actually run, and a reproducible package. Bilingual interface
  (Spanish and English). Every engine was checked against independent implementations in R
  and against the exhaustive enumeration of all trees.
